## Summary

The `isInternalNetwork()` function checks the hostname string against regex patterns for private IP ranges. However, it validates the **hostname** (which may be a domain name), not the **resolved IP address**. An attacker could register a domain that resolves to a private IP, bypassing the check entirely.

Additionally, IPv6-mapped IPv4 addresses (e.g., `::ffff:127.0.0.1`) and decimal IP notation (e.g., `2130706433` for `127.0.0.1`) are not blocked.

## Vulnerable Code

```typescript
// src/registry/discovery.ts
export function isInternalNetwork(hostname: string): boolean {
  const blocked = [
    /^127\./, /^10\./, /^172\.(1[6-9]|2\d|3[01])\./,
    /^192\.168\./, /^169\.254\./, /^::1$/, /^localhost$/i, /^0\./,
  ];
  return blocked.some(pattern => pattern.test(hostname));
}

export function isAllowedUri(uri: string): boolean {
  const url = new URL(uri);
  if (url.protocol === 'https:' && isInternalNetwork(url.hostname)) return false;
  // hostname "evil.attacker.com" passes, even if it resolves to 127.0.0.1
```

## Recommendation

1. Resolve DNS first using `dns.lookup()`, then validate the resolved IP
2. Block IPv6-mapped IPv4 addresses (`::ffff:` prefix)
3. Block decimal IP notation
4. Make `isAllowedUri` async to support DNS resolution

**Severity:** MEDIUM
**CWE:** CWE-918 (Server-Side Request Forgery)
**Location:** `src/registry/discovery.ts` — lines 33–57

